\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The criterion is a mechanism test, and the mechanism is measurable.} \textbf  {(a)} What C2 checks: the upstream transform $T$ and the statistic $S$ that $d_2$ reads determine an \emph  {invariance class} (Proposition~\ref {prop:invariance}), and the class determines the verdict---no composition needs to be run. This is why norm clipping does not blind FoolsGold but does blind Reputation. \textbf  {(b)} The measured chain for FG$\to $RFA, per round, on the transformed points RFA actually receives. Each marker is one round with $w_a>0$ (rounds where FoolsGold zeroes every adversary are Lemma~\ref {lem:annihilation} and carry no payload); large markers are mean~$\pm $~s.d. Corollary~\ref {cor:aggregate_mass}'s bound holds in every round, and the $\alpha {=}0.1$ collapse is visible as a collapse in the theorem's own quantity: $m$ falls from $4.71 \pm 1.51$ ($n{=}17$ rounds) to $2.06 \pm 2.35$ ($n{=}12$), with several rounds at $m{<}1$ where the bound degrades to $n_a/(n_b{+}n_a)$, and realized $\Lambda _a$ rises from $0.003$ to $0.085$ (max $0.50$) as composition ASR goes $0.093 \to 0.569$. Values below $10^{-4}$ are drawn at $10^{-4}$.}}{12}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanism}{{2}{12}{\textbf {The criterion is a mechanism test, and the mechanism is measurable.} \textbf {(a)} What C2 checks: the upstream transform $T$ and the statistic $S$ that $d_2$ reads determine an \emph {invariance class} (Proposition~\ref {prop:invariance}), and the class determines the verdict---no composition needs to be run. This is why norm clipping does not blind FoolsGold but does blind Reputation. \textbf {(b)} The measured chain for FG$\to $RFA, per round, on the transformed points RFA actually receives. Each marker is one round with $w_a>0$ (rounds where FoolsGold zeroes every adversary are Lemma~\ref {lem:annihilation} and carry no payload); large markers are mean~$\pm $~s.d. Corollary~\ref {cor:aggregate_mass}'s bound holds in every round, and the $\alpha {=}0.1$ collapse is visible as a collapse in the theorem's own quantity: $m$ falls from $4.71 \pm 1.51$ ($n{=}17$ rounds) to $2.06 \pm 2.35$ ($n{=}12$), with several rounds at $m{<}1$ where the bound degrades to $n_a/(n_b{+}n_a)$, and realized $\Lambda _a$ rises from $0.003$ to $0.085$ (max $0.50$) as composition ASR goes $0.093 \to 0.569$. Values below $10^{-4}$ are drawn at $10^{-4}$}{figure.caption.9}{}}
